Certainly! Here's an example of unit test cases for the ReactJS WebSocket application using the Jest testing framework:

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import { render, screen, fireEvent } from '@testing-library/react';
import WebSocketConnection from './WebSocketConnection';

describe('WebSocketConnection', () => {
  let originalWebSocket;

  beforeEach(() => {
    // Mock the WebSocket class
    originalWebSocket = global.WebSocket;
    global.WebSocket = jest.fn(() => ({
      send: jest.fn(),
      close: jest.fn(),
    }));
  });

  afterEach(() => {
    // Restore the original WebSocket class
    global.WebSocket = originalWebSocket;
  });

  test('renders WebSocketConnection component', () => {
    render(<WebSocketConnection />);
    const buttonElement = screen.getByText('Send Message');
    expect(buttonElement).toBeInTheDocument();
  });

  test('sends message when button is clicked', () => {
    render(<WebSocketConnection />);
    const buttonElement = screen.getByText('Send Message');
    fireEvent.click(buttonElement);

    // WebSocket send method should have been called with the message
    expect(global.WebSocket).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
    expect(global.WebSocket.mock.instances[0].send).toHaveBeenCalledWith('Hello, server!');
  });

  test('closes WebSocket connection on unmount', () => {
    const { unmount } = render(<WebSocketConnection />);
    unmount();

    // WebSocket close method should have been called
    expect(global.WebSocket.mock.instances[0].close).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
  });
});
```

In this example, we use the Jest testing framework along with the `@testing-library/react` package to render the `WebSocketConnection` component and simulate user interactions. 

In the first test case, we ensure that the component renders correctly by checking the presence of the "Send Message" button.

In the second test case, we simulate a button click and verify that the WebSocket's `send` method is called with the expected message.

In the third test case, we unmount the component and verify that the WebSocket's `close` method is called.

Make sure to install the necessary dependencies, including `jest`, `@testing-library/react`, and any other required testing libraries, and configure your project accordingly.

You can run the tests using the `npm test` command or your preferred test runner command.

Feel free to add more test cases based on your specific requirements and to cover different scenarios.

