   - Override the `onModuleLoad()` method, which acts as the entry point of the GWT application.

   ```java
   import com.google.gwt.core.client.EntryPoint;
   import com.google.gwt.user.client.ui.Button;
   import com.google.gwt.user.client.ui.Label;
   import com.google.gwt.user.client.ui.RootPanel;
   import com.google.gwt.user.client.ui.TextBox;
   import com.google.gwt.event.dom.client.ClickEvent;
   import com.google.gwt.event.dom.client.ClickHandler;

   public class MultiplyModule implements EntryPoint {

     public void onModuleLoad() {
       final TextBox textBox1 = new TextBox();
       final TextBox textBox2 = new TextBox();
       final Label resultLabel = new Label();

       Button multiplyButton = new Button("Multiply");
       multiplyButton.addClickHandler(new ClickHandler() {
         public void onClick(ClickEvent event) {
           int num1 = Integer.parseInt(textBox1.getValue());
           int num2 = Integer.parseInt(textBox2.getValue());
           int result = num1 * num2;
           resultLabel.setText("Result: " + result);
         }
       });

       RootPanel.get().add(textBox1);
       RootPanel.get().add(textBox2);
       RootPanel.get().add(multiplyButton);
       RootPanel.get().add(resultLabel);
     }
   }
   ```

   In this example, we create a GWT module called `MultiplyModule`. We use GWT widgets such as `TextBox`, `Button`, and `Label` to create the UI elements. When the "Multiply" button is clicked, we parse the values from the text boxes, perform the multiplication operation, and display the result in the `resultLabel` widget.

3. Compile the GWT module:
   - Use the GWT compiler to compile the GWT module into JavaScript and HTML files.

4. Set up the HTML page:
   - Create an HTML page (`Multiply.html`) to host the GWT application and include the compiled JavaScript file.

   ```html
   <!DOCTYPE html>
   <html>
     <head>
       <meta charset="UTF-8">
       <title>Multiply</title>
       <script type="text/javascript" src="multiply/multiply.nocache.js"></script>
     </head>
     <body>
       <h1>Multiply</h1>
     </body>
   </html>
   ```

5. Run the GWT application:
   - Deploy the GWT application on a web server or use GWT's development mode to run it locally.

When you open the `Multiply.html` page in a web browser, you'll see the "Multiply" UI with two text boxes, a button, and a label. Enter two numbers in the text boxes and click the button to see the multiplication result displayed in the label.

