Dependency Injection (DI) is a design pattern and a key feature of the Spring Framework, including Spring Boot. It is a technique used to achieve loose coupling and promote modular, reusable, and testable code.

In Spring Boot, dependency injection is achieved through the Inversion of Control (IoC) container, which manages the creation and lifecycle of objects (beans) and their dependencies.

With dependency injection, the responsibility of creating and managing dependencies is delegated to the IoC container. Instead of manually instantiating and wiring dependencies, you declare the dependencies of a class using annotations such as `@Autowired`, `@Inject`, or constructor injection, and the container resolves and injects the dependencies automatically at runtime.

Here's an example to illustrate dependency injection in Spring Boot:

```java
@Service
public class UserService {

    private final UserRepository userRepository;

    @Autowired
    public UserService(UserRepository userRepository) {
        this.userRepository = userRepository;
    }

    // ...
}
```

In the above code, the `UserService` class has a dependency on the `UserRepository` interface. By annotating the constructor with `@Autowired`, we indicate that Spring should inject an instance of `UserRepository` when creating an instance of `UserService`. Spring Boot's IoC container will automatically resolve the dependency and provide an instance of `UserRepository` to the constructor.

By using dependency injection, you decouple the classes from their dependencies, making them easier to test and maintain. It also enables better separation of concerns and promotes modular and reusable code.

Spring Boot provides various annotations and mechanisms for dependency injection, including constructor injection, field injection, and method injection. Additionally, you can use qualifiers, scopes, and other advanced features to further control and customize the injection process.

