```java
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient;

@SpringBootApplication
public class ReactiveRestApiConsumerApplication {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(ReactiveRestApiConsumerApplication.class, args);
    }

    @Bean
    public WebClient webClient() {
        return WebClient.create();
    }

    // Example method to consume a REST API
    public void consumeApi() {
        WebClient webClient = WebClient.create();

        String apiUrl = "https://api.example.com/data"; // Replace with the actual API URL

        // Make a GET request to the API and retrieve the response
        webClient.get()
                .uri(apiUrl)
                .retrieve()
                .bodyToMono(String.class)
                .subscribe(response -> {
                    // Process the response as needed
                    System.out.println("API Response: " + response);
                });
    }
}
```

In this example, we have a `ReactiveRestApiConsumerApplication` class annotated with `@SpringBootApplication`. We create a `WebClient` bean using the `WebClient.create()` method.

The `consumeApi()` method demonstrates how to consume a REST API using the reactive WebClient. Replace `https://api.example.com/data` with the actual URL of the REST API you want to consume. The `get()` method constructs a GET request, and `uri()` specifies the API URL. The `retrieve()` method performs the request and returns a response object. We then use `bodyToMono(String.class)` to deserialize the response body to a `Mono<String>`, which represents a single value emitted asynchronously.

To handle the response, we subscribe to the `Mono` and provide a lambda function that processes the response. In this example, we simply print the response, but you can customize the processing logic as needed.

