Certainly! Here's a Java 8 program that filters out the string values in an array that are not repeating (i.e., appear only once):

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.function.Function;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class FilterNonRepeatingStrings {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] array = {"apple", "banana", "cherry", "banana", "apple", "orange", "mango"};

        List<String> nonRepeatingStrings = Arrays.stream(array)
                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Function.identity(), Collectors.counting()))
                .entrySet()
                .stream()
                .filter(entry -> entry.getValue() == 1L)
                .map(Map.Entry::getKey)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());

        System.out.println("Non-repeating strings in the array: " + nonRepeatingStrings);
    }
}
```

In this program, we start with an array of strings. We use the Java 8 Stream API to perform the following operations:

1. `Arrays.stream(array)` - Converts the array into a stream of elements.
2. `.collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Function.identity(), Collectors.counting()))` - Groups the elements by their values and counts the occurrences of each value.
3. `.entrySet().stream()` - Converts the map entries into a stream of key-value pairs.
4. `.filter(entry -> entry.getValue() == 1L)` - Filters out the key-value pairs where the value is equal to 1 (i.e., the string appears only once).
5. `.map(Map.Entry::getKey)` - Maps the key-value pairs to their keys (the non-repeating strings).
6. `.collect(Collectors.toList())` - Collects the non-repeating strings into a new list.

Finally, we print the resulting list of non-repeating strings. In the example above, the output will be:

```
Non-repeating strings in the array: [cherry, orange, mango]
```

Please note that this program considers a string as repeating if it appears more than once in the array. If you have a different definition of repeating strings, you can modify the program accordingly.

When invoking an API, it's important to handle exceptions properly to handle any potential errors or failures. In the case of Spring Boot with WebClient, you can handle exceptions by using the `onErrorResume` operator or the `exchange` method with error handling.

Here's an example of how to handle exceptions when invoking an API using WebClient:

```java
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClientResponseException;

@SpringBootApplication
public class ReactiveRestApiConsumerApplication {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(ReactiveRestApiConsumerApplication.class, args);
    }

    @Bean
    public WebClient webClient() {
        return WebClient.create();
    }

    // Example method to consume a REST API with exception handling
    public void consumeApi() {
        WebClient webClient = WebClient.create();

        String apiUrl = "https://api.example.com/data"; // Replace with the actual API URL

        webClient.get()
                .uri(apiUrl)
                .retrieve()
                .bodyToMono(String.class)
                .onErrorResume(WebClientResponseException.class, ex -> {
                    // Handle specific HTTP status code errors
                    if (ex.getRawStatusCode() == 404) {
                        System.out.println("API not found: " + ex.getMessage());
                    } else if (ex.getRawStatusCode() == 500) {
                        System.out.println("API server error: " + ex.getMessage());
                    } else {
                        System.out.println("API error: " + ex.getMessage());
                    }

                    // Return a default value or fallback response
                    return Mono.just("Default response");
                })
                .subscribe(response -> {
                    // Process the response
                    System.out.println("API Response: " + response);
                });
    }
}
```

In this example, we added exception handling using the `onErrorResume` operator. Inside the `onErrorResume` block, you can specify how to handle specific exceptions or HTTP status codes. In this case, we check for common HTTP status codes like 404 and 500 and provide custom error handling logic. You can add additional conditions or customize the handling based on your specific requirements.

If an exception occurs during the API call, the `onErrorResume` block will be triggered, and the provided fallback response or default value will be returned. This allows you to handle errors gracefully and provide meaningful responses to your application.

Make sure to import the necessary classes (`WebClient`, `WebClientResponseException`, `Mono`) in your code.

Remember to replace the `apiUrl` with the actual URL of the API you want to invoke.

By properly handling exceptions, you can improve the resilience and error handling capabilities of your application when consuming REST APIs.

