```javascript
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import axios from 'axios';

function useApi() {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);
  const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(false);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);

  async function fetchData(url) {
    setIsLoading(true);
    setError(null);

    try {
      const response = await axios.get(url);
      setData(response.data);
    } catch (error) {
      setError('API request failed');
    }

    setIsLoading(false);
  }

  async function postData(url, body) {
    setIsLoading(true);
    setError(null);

    try {
      const response = await axios.post(url, body);
      setData(response.data);
    } catch (error) {
      setError('API request failed');
    }

    setIsLoading(false);
  }

  async function putData(url, body) {
    setIsLoading(true);
    setError(null);

    try {
      const response = await axios.put(url, body);
      setData(response.data);
    } catch (error) {
      setError('API request failed');
    }

    setIsLoading(false);
  }

  return { data, isLoading, error, fetchData, postData, putData };
}
```

In this example, the `useApi` custom hook returns an object with `data`, `isLoading`, `error`, `fetchData`, `postData`, and `putData` properties.

The `fetchData` function uses Axios to make a GET request to the specified URL and sets the response data in the `data` state. If an error occurs, it sets the `error` state.

The `postData` function uses Axios to make a POST request to the specified URL with the provided request body. Similarly, the `putData` function makes a PUT request.

To use the custom hook in your components, you can do something like this:

```javascript
import React, { useEffect } from 'react';
import useApi from './useApi';

function MyComponent() {
  const { data, isLoading, error, fetchData, postData, putData } = useApi();

  useEffect(() => {
    // Fetch data when the component mounts
    fetchData('https://api.example.com/data');
  }, [fetchData]);

  if (isLoading) {
    return <div>Loading...</div>;
  }

  if (error) {
    return <div>Error: {error}</div>;
  }

  return (
    <div>
      {/* Render the data */}
      <button onClick={() => postData('https://api.example.com/data', { key: 'value' })}>Post Data</button>
      <button onClick={() => putData('https://api.example.com/data', { key: 'updated value' })}>Put Data</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

In this example, the `MyComponent` renders the fetched data, displays a loading message while data is being fetched, and shows an error message if an error occurs. It also provides buttons to invoke the `postData` and `putData` functions with the appropriate URLs and request bodies.

By using the `useApi` custom hook, you can easily make API requests with different HTTP methods and handle the response data, loading state, and errors in your components.

