```java
import javax.persistence.*;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

@Entity
@Table(name = "node")
public class Node {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(name = "name")
    private String name;

    @ManyToMany
    @JoinTable(
            name = "node_relationship",
            joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "source_node_id"),
            inverseJoinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "target_node_id")
    )
    private Set<Node> relationships = new HashSet<>();

    // Constructors, getters, and setters

    public void addRelationship(Node node) {
        relationships.add(node);
        node.getRelationships().add(this);
    }

    public void removeRelationship(Node node) {
        relationships.remove(node);
        node.getRelationships().remove(this);
    }

    // Other methods as needed

    public Set<Node> getRelationships() {
        return relationships;
    }
}
```

In this updated version, we have added a `relationships` field of type `Set<Node>` to represent the many-to-many relationship between nodes. The `@ManyToMany` annotation is used to define the relationship, and the `@JoinTable` annotation specifies the intermediate table name and the columns used for the join.

The `addRelationship` method is used to add a relationship between two nodes. It adds the specified node to the current node's `relationships` set and vice versa.

Similarly, the `removeRelationship` method removes a relationship between two nodes by removing the specified node from the `relationships` set of both nodes.

