```

3. Create an instance of `RestTemplate` and use it to perform the GET request with query parameters:

```java
public class MyApiClient {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create a new instance of RestTemplate
        RestTemplate restTemplate = new RestTemplate();

        // Define the base URL and query parameters
        String baseUrl = "http://example.com/api/resource";
        MultiValueMap<String, String> queryParams = new LinkedMultiValueMap<>();
        queryParams.add("param1", "value1");
        queryParams.add("param2", "value2");

        // Perform the GET request with query parameters
        ResponseEntity<String> response = restTemplate.getForEntity(baseUrl, String.class, queryParams);

        // Handle the response
        if (response.getStatusCode().is2xxSuccessful()) {
            String responseBody = response.getBody();
            System.out.println("Response Body: " + responseBody);
        } else {
            System.err.println("Request failed with status code: " + response.getStatusCodeValue());
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we created a `RestTemplate` instance, defined the base URL (`baseUrl`), and set the query parameters (`queryParams`) using a `MultiValueMap`. We used `getForEntity()` method of `RestTemplate` to perform the GET request, passing the base URL and query parameters as arguments. The response is then stored in a `ResponseEntity`, and we can access the response body using `response.getBody()`.

Please note that you need to replace `http://example.com/api/resource` with the actual API URL you want to call, and also replace `param1`, `value1`, `param2`, `value2`, etc., with the actual query parameters and their corresponding values that you want to include in the request.

