Yes, you can also solve the CORS issue by setting up a proxy server. A proxy server acts as an intermediary between the client (your frontend application) and the backend server (http://192.168.15.121:8123). By routing your requests through the proxy, you can avoid the CORS restrictions enforced by the browser.

To set up a proxy server, you need to create a backend server (e.g., using Node.js, Express, or any other server-side technology) that will forward requests from your frontend application to the actual server at `http://192.168.15.121:8123`. The backend server can then relay the responses back to your frontend, effectively bypassing the CORS restrictions.

Here's an example of how you can achieve this using a Node.js and Express backend server:

1. Create a new Node.js project and install the required dependencies:
```bash
npm init -y
npm install express axios
```

2. Create a new file named `server.js` (or any other appropriate name) and add the following code:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const axios = require('axios');

const app = express();
const PORT = 3001; // Choose any available port for the proxy server

app.use(express.json());

// Proxy route to forward requests to http://192.168.15.121:8123
app.post('/proxy', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const url = 'http://192.168.15.121:8123' + req.url;
    const response = await axios({
      method: req.method,
      url,
      data: req.body,
    });

    res.json(response.data);
  } catch (error) {
    res.status(error.response?.status || 500).json(error.response?.data || { message: 'Something went wrong' });
  }
});

app.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Proxy server is running on port ${PORT}`);
});
```

3. Save the file and start the proxy server by running `node server.js`.

4. Update your frontend application to make requests to the proxy server instead of directly to `http://192.168.15.121:8123`. For example:

```javascript
const apiUrl = 'http://localhost:3001/proxy';

// Make a POST request to the backend through the proxy server
axios.post(apiUrl, {
  query: 'SELECT Country FROM ESG.SalesData',
})
.then(response => {
  // Handle the response from the backend server
})
.catch(error => {
  // Handle any errors
});
```

By sending your requests through the proxy server at `http://localhost:3001`, the browser's same-origin policy will not apply since the frontend and the backend are now on the same origin. The proxy server will then forward the requests to the actual backend server at `http://192.168.15.121:8123` and relay the responses back to your frontend application.

Remember to adjust the port number and the endpoint paths in the frontend and backend code to match your specific setup.

Using a proxy server can be a viable solution, especially when you don't have control over the backend server's CORS configuration or if you want to keep the CORS settings on the backend server strict for security reasons.

