This code will load the script specified by the selected option's value whenever the selection changes, and will handle the script loading with appropriate success and error messages.

The `Array.prototype.forEach` method in JavaScript doesn't support breaking out of the loop. If you need to break out of a loop, you should use a different loop structure such as a `for` loop or `Array.prototype.some` or `Array.prototype.every` which allow for breaking or returning early.

Here are a few alternatives:

### 1. Using a `for` loop
```javascript
const array = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

for (let i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
    if (array[i] === 3) {
        break; // Exit the loop when the condition is met
    }
    console.log(array[i]);
}
```

### 2. Using `Array.prototype.some`
The `some` method will stop iterating through the array once the callback returns `true`.

```javascript
const array = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

array.some(element => {
    if (element === 3) {
        return true; // Stop iteration
    }
    console.log(element);
    return false;
});
```

### 3. Using `Array.prototype.every`
The `every` method will stop iterating through the array once the callback returns `false`.

```javascript
const array = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

array.every(element => {
    if (element === 3) {
        return false; // Stop iteration
    }
    console.log(element);
    return true;
});
```

### Example Usage

