This approach ensures that each promise is processed sequentially, and the chain is stopped immediately upon encountering a rejection.

In JavaScript, you can't create interfaces in the same way you would in strongly-typed languages like TypeScript or Java. JavaScript is a dynamically-typed language and does not have built-in support for interfaces. However, you can simulate the concept of interfaces using various techniques. Here are some approaches:

### Using TypeScript

While this isn't pure JavaScript, TypeScript is a superset of JavaScript that adds static types, including interfaces. Here's an example of how you can define and use an interface in TypeScript:

```typescript
// Define an interface
interface Person {
    name: string;
    age: number;
    greet(): void;
}

// Implement the interface in a class
class Student implements Person {
    name: string;
    age: number;

    constructor(name: string, age: number) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }

    greet() {
        console.log(`Hello, my name is ${this.name} and I am ${this.age} years old.`);
    }
}

const student = new Student('Alice', 23);
student.greet(); // Output: Hello, my name is Alice and I am 23 years old.
```

### Using Plain JavaScript with JSDoc

You can use JSDoc annotations to simulate interfaces in JavaScript. This approach doesn't enforce types at runtime but can help with tooling and IDE support.

```javascript
/**
 * @interface
 */
function Person() {}

/**
 * @type {string}
 */
Person.prototype.name;

/**
 * @type {number}
 */
Person.prototype.age;

/**
 * @returns {void}
 */
Person.prototype.greet = function() {};

/**
 * @implements {Person}
 */
class Student {
    /**
     * @param {string} name
     * @param {number} age
     */
    constructor(name, age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }

    greet() {
        console.log(`Hello, my name is ${this.name} and I am ${this.age} years old.`);
    }
}

const student = new Student('Alice', 23);
student.greet(); // Output: Hello, my name is Alice and I am 23 years old.
```

### Using a Custom Interface-Checking Function

You can write a function to check if an object adheres to a certain structure at runtime. This approach is more manual and less common but can be useful in some scenarios.

```javascript
function implementsPerson(obj) {
    return (
        typeof obj.name === 'string' &&
        typeof obj.age === 'number' &&
        typeof obj.greet === 'function'
    );
}

class Student {
    constructor(name, age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }

    greet() {
        console.log(`Hello, my name is ${this.name} and I am ${this.age} years old.`);
    }
}

const student = new Student('Alice', 23);

if (implementsPerson(student)) {
    student.greet(); // Output: Hello, my name is Alice and I am 23 years old.
} else {
    console.error('The student does not implement the Person interface.');
}
```

### Summary

While JavaScript does not have built-in support for interfaces, you can use TypeScript for static type-checking and interfaces, JSDoc for improved tooling support, or custom runtime checks to ensure objects adhere to a specific structure. Each approach has its own use cases and benefits depending on the project requirements and the development environment.

