To style the new `div` with ID `id3` as a flex container, you can add CSS styles directly using JavaScript or by adding a `<style>` tag in your HTML. Below, I'll demonstrate both methods.

### Method 1: Using JavaScript

Add CSS styles directly to the `div3` element using JavaScript.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Append Divs Example</title>
</head>
<body>
    <div id="id1">This is the first div.</div>
    <div id="id2">This is the second div.</div>
    <script>
        // JavaScript to create and append the third div
        const div1 = document.getElementById('id1');
        const div2 = document.getElementById('id2');

        // Create the new div
        const div3 = document.createElement('div');
        div3.id = 'id3';

        // Style the new div as a flex container
        div3.style.display = 'flex';
        div3.style.flexDirection = 'row'; // You can change to 'column' if needed
        div3.style.gap = '10px'; // Optional: Adds space between flex items

        // Append existing divs to the new div
        div3.appendChild(div1);
        div3.appendChild(div2);

        // Append the new div to the body
        document.body.appendChild(div3);
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Method 2: Using a `<style>` Tag in HTML

Add a `<style>` tag in the `<head>` section of your HTML to apply CSS styles.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Append Divs Example</title>
    <style>
        #id3 {
            display: flex;
            flex-direction: row; /* Change to 'column' if needed */
            gap: 10px; /* Optional: Adds space between flex items */
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div id="id1">This is the first div.</div>
    <div id="id2">This is the second div.</div>
    <script>
        // JavaScript to create and append the third div
        const div1 = document.getElementById('id1');
        const div2 = document.getElementById('id2');

        // Create the new div
        const div3 = document.createElement('div');
        div3.id = 'id3';

        // Append existing divs to the new div
        div3.appendChild(div1);
        div3.appendChild(div2);

        // Append the new div to the body
        document.body.appendChild(div3);
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation

- **JavaScript Method**: The `style` property of `div3` is used to apply CSS directly to the element.
- **CSS Method**: A `<style>` tag is used to define CSS rules that target the `id3` element. This method separates concerns by keeping style definitions in CSS and behavior in JavaScript.

Both methods will style the `id3` div as a flex container, arranging its child elements (the `id1` and `id2` divs) in a row (or column if you change `flex-direction`).

