In JavaScript, when you modify a frozen object or an immutable object created with libraries like Immutable.js, the language typically doesn't throw errors or warnings in non-strict mode. Instead, the changes simply won't take effect. However, in strict mode, attempting to modify a frozen object will throw a `TypeError`.

### Enabling Strict Mode for Error Handling

To enable strict mode, add `"use strict";` at the top of your JavaScript file or function. This way, you'll get errors when trying to modify frozen objects.

```javascript
"use strict";

const API_URLS = Object.freeze({
  BASE_URL: 'https://api.example.com',
  LOGIN: 'https://api.example.com/login',
  SIGNUP: 'https://api.example.com/signup',
});

API_URLS.BASE_URL = 'https://api.changed.com'; // This will throw a TypeError
```

### Example with React and Strict Mode

Here's an example of using strict mode in a React component:

```javascript
import React from 'react';

"use strict";

const API_URLS = Object.freeze({
  BASE_URL: 'https://api.example.com',
  LOGIN: 'https://api.example.com/login',
  SIGNUP: 'https://api.example.com/signup',
});

const MyComponent = () => {
  try {
    API_URLS.BASE_URL = 'https://api.changed.com'; // This will throw a TypeError
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error modifying frozen object:', error);
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <p>API Base URL: {API_URLS.BASE_URL}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

export default MyComponent;
```

### Example with Immutable.js

Immutable.js doesn't throw errors by design when trying to modify immutable objects. Instead, it returns a new modified object, leaving the original unchanged. Therefore, you won't get runtime errors or warnings when trying to mutate immutable objects from Immutable.js.

Here's an example to illustrate:

```javascript
import { Map, List } from 'immutable';

const API_URLS = Map({
  BASE_URL: 'https://api.example.com',
  LOGIN: 'https://api.example.com/login',
  SIGNUP: 'https://api.example.com/signup',
});

const USER_ROLES = List(['Admin', 'Editor', 'Viewer']);

const newApiUrls = API_URLS.set('BASE_URL', 'https://api.changed.com'); // Returns a new Map
const newUserRoles = USER_ROLES.push('Guest'); // Returns a new List

console.log(newApiUrls.get('BASE_URL')); // https://api.changed.com
console.log(API_URLS.get('BASE_URL')); // https://api.example.com (original remains unchanged)
console.log(newUserRoles.toJS()); // ['Admin', 'Editor', 'Viewer', 'Guest']
console.log(USER_ROLES.toJS()); // ['Admin', 'Editor', 'Viewer'] (original remains unchanged)
```

### Conclusion

JavaScript does not natively throw errors when attempting to modify frozen objects unless in strict mode. Libraries like Immutable.js are designed to be immutable and do not throw errors; instead, they ensure immutability by returning new modified objects.

For stricter enforcement and error handling, always use `"use strict";` in your JavaScript code to catch potential mutations of frozen objects.

