     <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/github-markdown-css/5.5.1/github-markdown.min.css">
   </head>
   <body>
     <!-- Your content goes here -->
     <%- content %> <!-- This is where you render your Markdown content -->
   </body>
   </html>
   ```

2. **Include the CSS link in your response**: If you're serving Markdown content directly from your Node.js application (for example, as an API response), you can include the CSS link in the HTML response that includes your rendered Markdown content. Here's an example:

   ```javascript
   // Assuming you're using Express.js for your Node.js application
   const express = require('express');
   const marked = require('marked');
   const app = express();

   // Middleware to serve static files (like CSS)
   app.use(express.static('public'));

   // Route to render Markdown content
   app.get('/markdown', (req, res) => {
     // Render Markdown content (you would fetch or generate your Markdown content here)
     const markdownContent = '# My Markdown Content\n\nThis is *some* **Markdown** content.';
     const htmlContent = marked(markdownContent);

     // Construct the HTML response with the Markdown content and the CSS link
     const responseHtml = `
       <!DOCTYPE html>
       <html lang="en">
       <head>
         <meta charset="UTF-8">
         <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
         <title>Markdown with GitHub Markdown CSS</title>
         <!-- Link to the GitHub Markdown CSS hosted on the CDN -->
         <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/github-markdown-css/5.5.1/github-markdown.min.css">
       </head>
       <body>
         <div class="markdown-body">${htmlContent}</div>
       </body>
       </html>
     `;

     // Send the HTML response
     res.send(responseHtml);
   });

   // Start the server
   const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;
   app.listen(PORT, () => {
     console.log(`Server is running on port ${PORT}`);
   });
   ```

In both cases, the GitHub Markdown CSS link is included in the HTML response or template, ensuring that the rendered Markdown content is styled according to GitHub's Markdown styles.

