To apply your own CSS styles to the output generated by the `marked` method in Node.js, you can create a custom CSS file with your desired styles and then link it to your HTML document. Here's how you can do it:

1. Create a CSS file with your custom styles. For example, you can create a file named `custom-markdown.css`:

```css
/* Custom styles for Markdown content */
/* Add your custom CSS styles here */
```

2. Link your custom CSS file to your HTML document where you're rendering the Markdown content. You can do this using the `<link>` tag in the `<head>` section of your HTML document:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Markdown with Custom CSS</title>
  <!-- Link to your custom CSS file -->
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="custom-markdown.css">
</head>
<body>
  <!-- Your Markdown content goes here -->
</body>
</html>
```

3. When rendering Markdown content using the `marked` method in Node.js, you can specify options to include HTML `<style>` tags with your custom CSS styles. Here's how you can do it:

```javascript
const marked = require('marked');
const fs = require('fs');

// Read the Markdown content from a file
const markdownContent = fs.readFileSync('example.md', 'utf8');

// Options for the marked method
const options = {
  // Include HTML <style> tag with custom CSS styles
  renderer: new marked.Renderer(),
  gfm: true,
  breaks: true,
  pedantic: false,
  smartLists: true,
  smartypants: true,
  highlight: function (code, language) {
    const highlightedCode = language ? hljs.highlight(language, code).value : hljs.highlightAuto(code).value;
    return `<pre><code class="hljs">${highlightedCode}</code></pre>`;
  },
  // Include your custom CSS styles here
  headerPrefix: '<style>' + fs.readFileSync('custom-markdown.css', 'utf8') + '</style>'
};

// Render Markdown content with custom CSS styles
const htmlContent = marked(markdownContent, options);

// Output the HTML content
console.log(htmlContent);
```

In this code:

- Replace `'example.md'` with the path to your Markdown file.
- Replace `'custom-markdown.css'` with the path to your custom CSS file.
- The `headerPrefix` option is used to include the custom CSS styles within HTML `<style>` tags when rendering Markdown content with `marked`.
- Adjust the paths and file names as needed based on your project structure.

