This ensures that TypeScript knows the exact types of `message` and `index`, resolving the implicit `any` type error.

To complete the `orElseThrow()` method in the `StringOperation` lambda, you need to specify what exception should be thrown when the `Optional` is empty. In this case, you can use the `InvalidInputException` to provide a meaningful message.

Here’s the completed `toUpperCase` and `toLowerCase` definitions:

```java
public static final StringOperation toUpperCase = input ->
        input.map(String::toUpperCase)
             .orElseThrow(() -> new InvalidInputException("Input string is null or empty"));

public static final StringOperation toLowerCase = input ->
        input.map(String::toLowerCase)
             .orElseThrow(() -> new InvalidInputException("Input string is null or empty"));
```

### Explanation:
- **`orElseThrow`**: This method is used to throw an exception if the `Optional` is empty. You need to provide a `Supplier` for the exception, which is a lambda that returns a new instance of the exception.
- **`InvalidInputException`**: This exception is used to indicate that the input was invalid. The provided message will be passed to the exception’s constructor.

Here’s the complete code with these changes:

```java
package com.coding.practice.codes.stringOperations;

import java.util.Optional;

public class StringOperationsV5 {

    public static final StringOperation toUpperCase = input ->
            input.map(String::toUpperCase)
                 .orElseThrow(() -> new InvalidInputException("Input string is null or empty"));

    public static final StringOperation toLowerCase = input ->
            input.map(String::toLowerCase)
                 .orElseThrow(() -> new InvalidInputException("Input string is null or empty"));

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            Optional<String> sampleString = Optional.of("Premendra Kumar");
            System.out.println(toUpperCase.perform(sampleString));
            System.out.println(toLowerCase.perform(sampleString));
            System.out.println(toUpperCase.performWithTitle("Post Capitalization", sampleString));
            System.out.println(toLowerCase.performWithTitle("Post Making String in LowerCase", sampleString));
        } catch (InvalidInputException ex) {
            System.err.println("Error: " + ex.getMessage());
        } catch (Exception ex) {
            ex.printStackTrace();
        }
    }

    @FunctionalInterface
    private interface StringOperation {
        String perform(Optional<String> input) throws InvalidInputException;

        default String performWithTitle(String title, Optional<String> input) throws InvalidInputException {
            if (title != null) {
                System.out.println(title);
            }
            if (input.isEmpty()) {
                throw new InvalidInputException("Input is missing or empty");
            }
            return perform(input);
        }
    }

    private static class InvalidInputException extends Exception {
        public InvalidInputException(String message) {
            super(message);
        }
    }
}
```

### Summary:
- **`orElseThrow`** is used to throw an exception when the `Optional` is empty.
- **Exception Supplier**: A lambda function provides a new instance of `InvalidInputException` with an appropriate message.
- **Robust Handling**: This approach ensures that missing or empty inputs are handled consistently and clearly.

