To call an AWS Step Function from a Java program, you'll need to use the AWS SDK for Java. The AWS SDK provides a Java client for Step Functions, allowing you to interact with Step Functions and start executions.

Before you begin, make sure you have set up your AWS credentials and have the AWS SDK for Java added to your project dependencies.

Here's a basic Java program to call an AWS Step Function:

```java
import com.amazonaws.auth.DefaultAWSCredentialsProviderChain;
import com.amazonaws.client.builder.AwsClientBuilder;
import com.amazonaws.services.stepfunctions.AWSStepFunctions;
import com.amazonaws.services.stepfunctions.AWSStepFunctionsClientBuilder;
import com.amazonaws.services.stepfunctions.model.StartExecutionRequest;
import com.amazonaws.services.stepfunctions.model.StartExecutionResult;

public class StepFunctionCaller {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Replace these values with your actual region and Step Function ARN
        String region = "us-east-1";
        String stateMachineArn = "arn:aws:states:us-east-1:123456789012:stateMachine:MyStateMachine";

        // Create an instance of the Step Functions client
        AWSStepFunctions stepFunctionsClient = AWSStepFunctionsClientBuilder.standard()
                .withCredentials(DefaultAWSCredentialsProviderChain.getInstance())
                .withEndpointConfiguration(new AwsClientBuilder.EndpointConfiguration(
                        "https://states." + region + ".amazonaws.com", region))
                .build();

        // Input data for the Step Function (if needed)
        String input = "{}"; // Replace this with your actual input data in JSON format

        // Start the Step Function execution
        StartExecutionRequest request = new StartExecutionRequest()
                .withStateMachineArn(stateMachineArn)
                .withInput(input);

        StartExecutionResult result = stepFunctionsClient.startExecution(request);

        // Print the execution ARN (you can use this to track the execution status)
        System.out.println("Execution ARN: " + result.getExecutionArn());
    }
}
```

Remember to replace the `region` and `stateMachineArn` variables with the appropriate values for your AWS setup. The `input` variable can be used to pass any input data that your Step Function may require in JSON format.

Additionally, ensure that the IAM credentials used by the AWS SDK have sufficient permissions to invoke the Step Function.

With this program, you should be able to start the execution of your AWS Step Function from your Java application.

