If the Spring Boot server is running on a different domain or port than your React application, you may encounter Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) restrictions. CORS is a security mechanism implemented by web browsers to prevent cross-origin requests by default.

To handle CORS and make requests to a different domain or port, you can configure CORS settings in your Spring Boot application. Here's an example of how to enable CORS for all endpoints in Spring Boot:

1. Add the `@CrossOrigin` annotation to your controller class or specific handler methods. This annotation allows requests from all origins.

   ```java
   import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.CrossOrigin;
   import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
   import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

   @RestController
   @CrossOrigin
   public class StreamController {

     @GetMapping("/stream")
     public ResponseEntity<String> streamResponse() {
         // Your code here
     }
   }
   ```

   Alternatively, you can specify the allowed origins explicitly by providing a comma-separated list of domains as the `value` parameter of the `@CrossOrigin` annotation. For example:

   ```java
   @CrossOrigin(value = {"http://localhost:3000", "http://example.com"})
   ```

2. In your Spring Boot application, you can also configure CORS globally by creating a `CorsConfiguration` bean and adding it to the `WebMvcConfigurer`.

   ```java
   import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
   import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
   import org.springframework.web.servlet.config.annotation.CorsRegistry;
   import org.springframework.web.servlet.config.annotation.WebMvcConfigurer;
   import org.springframework.web.servlet.config.annotation.WebMvcConfigurerAdapter;
   import org.springframework.web.servlet.config.annotation.WebMvcConfigurerAdapter;

   @Configuration
   public class CorsConfig {

     @Bean
     public WebMvcConfigurer corsConfigurer() {
         return new WebMvcConfigurerAdapter() {
             @Override
             public void addCorsMappings(CorsRegistry registry) {
                 registry.addMapping("/**")
                         .allowedOrigins("http://localhost:3000", "http://example.com")
                         .allowedMethods("GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE");
             }
         };
     }
   }
   ```

   In the above example, the `addCorsMappings` method is overridden to configure allowed origins (`allowedOrigins`) and allowed HTTP methods (`allowedMethods`) for all endpoints (`"/**"`).

By configuring CORS settings in your Spring Boot application, you can allow cross-origin requests from your React application running on a different domain or port. This will enable your React application to make requests to the Spring Boot server without encountering CORS restrictions.

To send a stream response with Spring Boot, you can utilize the `ResponseEntity` class along with `StreamingResponseBody` or `ResponseEntity.BodyBuilder` to stream the response content. Here's an example:

```java
import org.springframework.core.io.InputStreamResource;
import org.springframework.http.HttpHeaders;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.MediaType;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Controller;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;

import java.io.FileInputStream;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStream;

@Controller
public class StreamController {

    @GetMapping("/stream")
    public ResponseEntity<InputStreamResource> streamResponse() throws IOException {
        InputStream inputStream = new FileInputStream("path/to/your/file");

        // Create the InputStreamResource from the input stream
        InputStreamResource inputStreamResource = new InputStreamResource(inputStream);

        // Set the response headers
        HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
        headers.setContentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_OCTET_STREAM);
        headers.setContentDispositionFormData("attachment", "filename.txt");

        // Create the ResponseEntity with the InputStreamResource and headers
        return ResponseEntity
                .status(HttpStatus.OK)
                .headers(headers)
                .body(inputStreamResource);
    }
}
```

In this example, the `/stream` endpoint returns a file stream response. You can replace `"path/to/your/file"` with the actual path to the file you want to stream.

Key points to note in the code:

1. The `InputStreamResource` wraps the input stream to be streamed as the response content.

2. The `HttpHeaders` are set to specify the content type (`MediaType.APPLICATION_OCTET_STREAM` in this example) and the content disposition (attachment or inline) with the file name.

3. The `ResponseEntity` is built using the `status`, `headers`, and `body` methods. The `InputStreamResource` is set as the body, and the appropriate status (e.g., `HttpStatus.OK`) is returned.

When a client requests the `/stream` endpoint, the file stream will be sent as the response, and the client can handle it accordingly (e.g., download the file or display it in the browser, depending on the content disposition header).

Make sure to adapt the code to suit your specific requirements and file handling scenarios.

