The `List.of` method in Java creates an immutable list and does not allow null values. If you try to include null values, it will throw a `NullPointerException`. To work around this, you can use a mutable list such as `ArrayList` which allows null values.

Here is the revised example using `ArrayList`:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class NullFilter {

    /**
     * Filters out null values from a list of objects.
     *
     * @param list the list of objects
     * @param <T>  the type of objects in the list
     * @return a list containing only non-null objects from the input list
     */
    public <T> List<T> filterNulls(List<T> list) {
        return list.stream()
                   .filter(item -> item != null)
                   .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NullFilter filter = new NullFilter();
        List<String> strings = new ArrayList<>();
        strings.add("apple");
        strings.add(null);
        strings.add("banana");
        strings.add(null);
        strings.add("cherry");

        List<String> filteredStrings = filter.filterNulls(strings);
        System.out.println(filteredStrings); // Output: [apple, banana, cherry]
    }
}
```

In this code:
- `new ArrayList<>()` is used to create a mutable list that can contain null values.
- The `filterNulls` method works as before to filter out the null values from the list.

