To store a many-to-many relationship between entities in MySQL using Spring Boot JPA, you can follow these steps:

1. Define the Entities: Create the two entities that participate in the many-to-many relationship. Let's take an example of two entities, "Student" and "Course," where a student can enroll in multiple courses and a course can have multiple students.

```java
@Entity
public class Student {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private String name;

    @ManyToMany(cascade = CascadeType.ALL)
    @JoinTable(name = "student_course",
               joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "student_id"),
               inverseJoinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "course_id"))
    private Set<Course> courses = new HashSet<>();

    // constructors, getters, and setters
}

@Entity
public class Course {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private String name;

    @ManyToMany(mappedBy = "courses")
    private Set<Student> students = new HashSet<>();

    // constructors, getters, and setters
}
```

2. Set up the Database Configuration: Configure the database connection properties in your Spring Boot `application.properties` or `application.yml` file.

3. Create the Repository Interfaces: Create repository interfaces for both entities by extending the `JpaRepository`.

```java
public interface StudentRepository extends JpaRepository<Student, Long> {
}

public interface CourseRepository extends JpaRepository<Course, Long> {
}
```

4. Perform Database Operations: Use these repository interfaces in your service or controller classes to perform database operations.

For example, to save a new student with enrolled courses:

```java
@Service
public class StudentService {
    private final StudentRepository studentRepository;
    private final CourseRepository courseRepository;

    public StudentService(StudentRepository studentRepository, CourseRepository courseRepository) {
        this.studentRepository = studentRepository;
        this.courseRepository = courseRepository;
    }

    public void enrollStudentInCourses(Long studentId, Set<Long> courseIds) {
        Student student = studentRepository.findById(studentId).orElseThrow(EntityNotFoundException::new);
        Set<Course> courses = courseRepository.findAllById(courseIds);

        student.getCourses().addAll(courses);
        studentRepository.save(student);
    }
}
```

In this example, `enrollStudentInCourses` method fetches the student by ID, fetches the courses by their IDs, adds the courses to the student's `courses` collection, and then saves the student.

By following these steps, you can store many-to-many relationship entities in MySQL using Spring Boot JPA.

